WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.345
Learning, for most people,

2
00:00:05.345 --> 00:00:09.255
involves a complex balancing of many different tasks.

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:11.050
A good way for you to keep

4
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.220
perspective about what you're trying to learn and accomplish is

5
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:20.150
to once a week write a brief weekly list of key tasks in a planner journal.

6
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.740
Then, each day on another page of your planner journal,

7
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.315
write a list of the tasks that you can reasonably work on or accomplish.

8
00:00:28.315 --> 00:00:31.845
Try to write this daily task list the evening before.

9
00:00:31.845 --> 00:00:33.750
Why the evening before?

10
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.190
Research has shown that this helps your

11
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.530
subconscious to grapple with the tasks on the list,

12
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.080
so you can figure out how to accomplish them.

13
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.500
Writing the list before you go to sleep enlists

14
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.460
your zombies to help you accomplish the items on the list the next day.

15
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.150
If you don't write your tasks down on a list,

16
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.570
they lurk at the edge of the four or so slots in your working memory,

17
00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.495
taking up valuable mental real estate.

18
00:00:59.495 --> 00:01:01.415
But once you make a task list,

19
00:01:01.415 --> 00:01:04.725
it frees working memory for problem-solving.

20
00:01:04.725 --> 00:01:07.580
So, let's look one of my daily task lists.

21
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.955
As you can see here,

22
00:01:08.955 --> 00:01:10.735
there are only six items.

23
00:01:10.735 --> 00:01:12.685
Some are process-oriented.

24
00:01:12.685 --> 00:01:16.560
For example, I have a paper due to a journal in several months,

25
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.735
so I spend a little focus time on most days working towards completing it.

26
00:01:21.735 --> 00:01:25.560
A few items are product-oriented but that is

27
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.475
only because they are doable within a limited period of time.

28
00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:32.045
Note my reminders.

29
00:01:32.045 --> 00:01:37.320
I wanted to keep my focus on each item when I'm working on it and I want to have fun.

30
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.775
I did catch myself getting sidetracked because I forgot to shut down my email.

31
00:01:41.775 --> 00:01:43.570
To get myself back into gear,

32
00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:49.805
I set a 22-minute Pomodoro challenge using a timer on my computer desktop.

33
00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:51.835
Why 22 minutes?

34
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:56.520
Well, why not? I don't have to do the same thing each time and notice, too,

35
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.160
that by moving to Pomodoro mode,

36
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.370
I've switched to a process orientation.

37
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:07.760
None of the items on my list is too big because I've got other things going on in my day;

38
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.020
meetings to go to,

39
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.390
a lecture to give.

40
00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:13.950
Sometimes I sprinkle a few tasks that involve physical motion on

41
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.710
my list even if it's just cleaning something which,

42
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.625
I'll admit, isn't ordinarily one of my favorite things to do.

43
00:02:21.625 --> 00:02:25.460
Somehow, because I'm using them as diffuse mode breaks,

44
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.320
I often look forward to them.

45
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.800
Mixing other tasks up with your learning seems to make everything more

46
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.755
enjoyable and keeps you from prolonged and unhealthy bouts of sitting.

47
00:02:36.755 --> 00:02:40.075
Over time, as I've gained more experience,

48
00:02:40.075 --> 00:02:45.335
I've gotten much better at gauging how long it takes to do any given task.

49
00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:48.390
You'll find yourself improving quickly as you become more

50
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.370
realistic about what you can reasonably do in any given time.

51
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:57.120
Make notes in your planner journal about what works and what doesn't.

52
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.415
Notice my goal finish time for the day - 5 pm.

53
00:03:01.415 --> 00:03:03.505
Doesn't seem quite right, does it?

54
00:03:03.505 --> 00:03:05.610
But it is right and it's one of

55
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.210
the most important components of your daily planner journal.

56
00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:14.450
Planning your quitting time is as important as planning your working time.

57
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.525
Generally, I aim to quit at 5 pm.

58
00:03:17.525 --> 00:03:19.230
Although when I'm learning something new,

59
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.065
it can sometimes be a pleasure to look at it again

60
00:03:22.065 --> 00:03:25.465
after I've taken an evening break just before I go to sleep.

61
00:03:25.465 --> 00:03:29.865
And occasionally, there's a major project that I'm wrapping up like,

62
00:03:29.865 --> 00:03:34.600
say, this MOOC, that has me running into a bit of overtime.

63
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.830
You might think, "Well, yeah.

64
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.020
But you're a professor who's",

65
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.130
shall we say, "past your youthful study days.

66
00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.660
Of course, an early quitting time is fine for you."

67
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.660
However, One of my most admired study experts,

68
00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:54.015
Cal Newport, used 5 pm quitting time through most of his student career.

69
00:03:54.015 --> 00:03:57.060
He ended up getting his PhD from MIT.

70
00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.100
In other words, this method,

71
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.625
implausible though it may seem for some,

72
00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:07.634
can work for undergraduate and graduate students in rigorous academic programs.

73
00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:13.590
Time after time, those who are committed to maintaining healthy leisure time along with

74
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:20.085
their hard work outperformed those who doggedly pursue an endless treadmill.

75
00:04:20.085 --> 00:04:25.870
Of course, your life may not lend itself to such a schedule with breaks and leisure time.

76
00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.500
You may be running on fumes with two jobs and too many classes.

77
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.700
But however your life is going,

78
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.750
try to squeeze a little break time in. One more thing.

79
00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.800
As writing coach, Daphne Gray-Grant,

80
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.915
recommends to her writing clients.

81
00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:43.435
Eat your frogs first in the morning.

82
00:04:43.435 --> 00:04:48.735
Try to work on a most important and most disliked task first.

83
00:04:48.735 --> 00:04:52.190
At least just one Pomodoro as soon as you wake up.

84
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.115
This is incredibly effective.

85
00:04:55.115 --> 00:05:00.270
You need to sometimes make changes in your plans because of unforeseen events,

86
00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:04.905
of course, but remember the law of serendipity.

87
00:05:04.905 --> 00:05:07.964
Lady Luck favors the one who tries.

88
00:05:07.964 --> 00:05:10.680
Planning well is part of trying.

89
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.920
Keep your eye on your learning goal and try not to

90
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.670
get too unsettled by occasional roadblocks.

91
00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.390
I'm Barbara Oakley.

92
00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.390
Thanks for learning how to learn.